Stored credential HTTP connectivity outcomes
===========================================

The stored credential test endpoint performs a generic GET to the target's
root path. That request cannot establish vendor authentication: a login page,
public landing page or redirect can answer independently of supplied secrets.
Previously every HTTP response below 500, including 401, 403 and 404, was
recorded as success. The UI then said that the credential had been validated.

A received HTTP 2xx now reports success for the HTTP connectivity check only,
stores last_test_result=reachable, and explicitly says authentication was not
verified. Redirects, client errors and server errors report success=false and
store failed. Every received HTTP response includes its status, scheme and
TLS verification setting, plus check_kind=http_connectivity and
 authentication_verified=false in device_info. Redirects are never followed.
The response schema remains compatible; the operation description is updated.
Historical success values are not rewritten. Clients inspecting the stored
free-form result should distinguish reachable from authenticated.

A completed HTTPS response, including HTTP 500/503, ends the check. It never
causes a retry over plaintext. Existing transport-error fallback remains
available only with allow_plaintext_http=true. Such an explicit opt-in can
send the stored username/password without encryption and should be avoided.
TLS verification remains enabled by default. Existing explicit verify_ssl=false
is unchanged; it is not a secure default or a substitute for a trusted CA.

The credentials UI shows the backend diagnostic and uses truthful fallback
messages in all three credential locale bundles (English, Spanish, Chinese).
The dialog explains that this check does not validate authentication or test
SSH, SNMP, API keys or tokens. Unsupported vendor authentication is not silently
advertised as complete. Actual driver-specific authentication needs separate
implementation and qualification; driver_id is not dispatched by this route.

Evidence and limits
-------------------
38 new PostgreSQL/API cases reproduce 28 old outcome failures and retain ten
permission/target denial controls. Related tests include all credential input,
site permission, storage, migration and unchanged TLS-default regressions.
The outbound client in these regression tests is a controlled double.

A separate probe runs 42 cases per source version with actual PostgreSQL,
incoming TCP HTTP and outgoing TCP HTTP/TLS. It checks status outcomes, stored
rows, synthetic Basic Authentication receipt, redirect refusal, tenant/site
and private-address denials, untrusted certificate rejection, explicit TLS
opt-out and explicitly enabled transport fallback. Only fixture-owned private
addresses and generated certificates/secrets are used. This is not vendor
hardware authentication or a real user login. Source hashes bind the proof.

Eight new rendered UI cases check visible outcomes and pre-submit limitations;
the two existing deletion-toast tests remain unchanged. Fresh frontend lint,
tests and strict build are required, followed by shipping/native gates.
Initial fixture stops are retained privately: strict X.509 verification
correctly rejected incomplete fixture certificates; a UI test hook returned
a mock and was incorrectly treated as a cleanup callback. Neither fix relaxed
application policy or removed assertions.

This correction does not qualify hostname TLS/SNI or IPv6 handling, response
body size limits, environment proxy isolation, driver-specific authentication,
or caller/session/API-key/grant revocation during execution. The independent
probe clears proxy environment settings as fixture containment, not as proof
that the application's client ignores those settings. No customer device is
contacted, no historic data is repaired, and no enterprise-readiness claim is
made by these focused results.
